const body = `<h1>2D Array Algorithms</h1>

<p>
    CED topic 4.13: implement algorithms over 2D arrays. FRQ 4's whole
    personality. Nearly every 2D algorithm is a 1D algorithm you already own,
    wearing a second loop like a jacket. The new ideas are only three: how to
    walk a grid, how to visit neighbors, and how to handle the edges.
</p>

<h2>The Standard Walks</h2>

<pre><code>// Row-major: left to right, top to bottom. The default.
for (int r = 0; r &lt; grid.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; grid[r].length; c++)
        visit(grid[r][c]);

// Column-major: top to bottom, column by column.
for (int c = 0; c &lt; grid[0].length; c++)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; grid.length; r++)
        visit(grid[r][c]);</code></pre>

<p>Two details the exam checks: <code>grid.length</code> is the number of ROWS,
and <code>grid[r].length</code> is the length of row r. Use the per-row form
always, and jagged arrays (rows of different lengths, like the maps in 8.6)
stop being a threat.</p>

<h2>The Catalog: 1D Ideas in 2D</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>1D ancestor</th><th>2D shape</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Sum / average the grid</td><td>accumulate</td><td>one total, one
        count, row-major walk</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Row sums / column sums</td><td>accumulate</td><td>outer loop over
        rows, inner accumulate; or an int[] of sums</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Find max and WHERE</td><td>max-and-index</td><td>track bestValue,
        bestR, bestC. Three variables, one if.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Count a value</td><td>count-by-category</td><td>counter, walk,
        compare</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Locate a value</td><td>linear search</td><td>return {r, c} or
        print; walk and compare</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Search a specific row</td><td>linear search</td><td>loop c over
        grid[r].length only. A row IS a 1D array.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Transpose / mirror</td><td>copy with transform</td><td>write into a
        new grid at flipped coordinates</td></tr>
    <tr><td>neighbors-of-a-cell</td><td>(new)</td><td>the 1D world has no
        neighbors. See below.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Neighbors: The New Idea</h2>

<p>Every cell in a grid has up to four orthogonal neighbors: up, down, left,
right. The problem is not the neighbors. It is the EDGE: corner and border
cells have fewer than four, and <code>grid[-1]</code> crashes.</p>

<pre><code>// The guard-per-direction approach (explicit, exam-friendly)
int up    = (r &gt; 0)              ? grid[r-1][c] : -1;
int down  = (r &lt; grid.length-1)   ? grid[r+1][c] : -1;
int left  = (c &gt; 0)              ? grid[r][c-1] : -1;
int right = (c &lt; grid[r].length-1)? grid[r][c+1] : -1;</code></pre>

<p>Using -1 as "no neighbor" works for maps (no wall has value -1). For counts
and sums, the other idiom is cleaner: check the bounds first, act second:</p>

<pre><code>// count living neighbors (Conway / fog of war style)
int count = 0;
if (r &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; grid[r-1][c] == 1) count++;
if (r &lt; grid.length-1 &amp;&amp; grid[r+1][c] == 1) count++;
if (c &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; grid[r][c-1] == 1) count++;
if (c &lt; grid[r].length-1 &amp;&amp; grid[r][c+1] == 1) count++;</code></pre>

<h2>The Diagonal Extension</h2>

<p>Eight neighbors instead of four doubles the cases. The professional trick is
a direction loop:</p>

<pre><code>int[] dr = {-1, -1, -1,  0, 0,  1, 1, 1};
int[] dc = {-1,  0,  1, -1, 1, -1, 0, 1};

int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i++) {
    int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];
    if (nr &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; nr &lt; grid.length &amp;&amp;
        nc &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; nc &lt; grid[nr].length &amp;&amp;
        grid[nr][nc] == 1) count++;
}</code></pre>

<p>Same guard, one place, eight directions. When you can, factor repetition
into data. This pattern generalizes to pathfinding (Season 2's maze solver)
where "neighbors" becomes "the cells you can move to."</p>

<h2>Partial Traversal: Bands and Boxes</h2>

<pre><code>// One row only: it is just a 1D array
for (int c = 0; c &lt; grid[r].length; c++) ...

// A box from (r1,c1) to (r2,c2), inclusive: nested loops with custom bounds
for (int r = r1; r &lt;= r2; r++)
    for (int c = c1; c &lt;= c2; c++)
        visit(grid[r][c]);</code></pre>

<p>Box traversal is the AoE spell: "damage everything in a 3x3 area around the
target." Custom bounds are just bounds; the walk does not care where the
numbers came from.</p>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Build the FRQ 4 Repertoire</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Five Methods, One Grid</h3>
    <p>Load <a href="../../data/dungeon1.txt">dungeon1.txt</a> (8.6 pattern) and
    write, each from scratch:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><code>int countWalls()</code> — count-by-value</li>
        <li><code>int[] rowFloorCounts()</code> — an array of per-row floor
        counts (row sums in disguise)</li>
        <li><code>int[] findPlayer()</code> — locate '@', return {r, c}</li>
        <li><code>int openNeighbors(int r, int c)</code> — how many of the 4
        neighbors are floor, with edge guards</li>
        <li><code>void fireball(int r, int c)</code> — turn every floor cell in
        the 3x3 box around (r,c) into '*' (burnt). Box traversal plus a write.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Then run fireball on the player's position, print the map, and check by
    eye that exactly the right cells burned. Visual verification is the best
    verification.</p>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>Three Grid Bugs</h3>
    <pre><code>// A: swapped bounds — compiles on square grids, crashes on wide ones
for (int r = 0; r &lt; grid[0].length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; grid.length; c++)

// B: the infinite mirror — writes while reading
for (r, c over grid) if (grid[r][c] == '.') grid[r][c] = '#';
// then in the SAME pass: if (grid[r][c] == '#') count++;</code></pre>
    <p>A: the loop variables are crossed with the bounds. On a square grid it
    accidentally works, which is the worst kind of bug: it hides until the map
    is rectangular. Fix by making bounds match variables. B: the count
    in the same pass counts walls you just created. Order of operations across
    a traversal is a real semantics question: count in a separate pass.</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why is grid.length the rows?</h3>
    <p>Because a 2D array is an array OF row-arrays. grid.length asks the outer
    array "how many do you hold?" and it holds rows. Each grid[r] is itself an
    array, whose own .length is its column count. The type system is the
    mnemonic: int[][] is "array of int[]," so the first length is the outer
    count. Jagged rows follow naturally from this picture, which is why 8.6
    taught per-row bounds.</p>
    <h3>Why does FRQ 4 love neighbors so much?</h3>
    <p>Because neighbors combine every skill at once: traversal, bounds
    guarding, conditionals, and state mutation. A question about "count
    adjacent occupied seats" or "spread fire to neighbors" tests whether you
    can think in two dimensions AND respect edges. It is the hardest thing to
    fake, which makes it perfect for a 6-point question.</p>
</div>

